  "type": "elizaosDailySummary",
  "title": "Daily Report - 2026-08-11",
  "categories": {
    "title": "ElizaOS Token Controversy and Community Discussion",
    "content": [
      {
        "text": "Community discussion on August 11, 2026 centered heavily on the ElizaOS token situation following a CoinDesk interview with Eliza Labs founder Shaw Walters. In the interview, Shaw acknowledged that the original AI16Z token was a memecoin, and described the migration to ElizaOS as an attempt to move beyond that status and carry the project vision forward. Shaw also revealed he sold his entire ElizaOS token holdings, which had once been worth approximately 25 million dollars but had collapsed in value to around 40,000 dollars at the time of sale, citing tax obligations as the reason.",
        "sources": "https://discord.com/channels/1253563208833433701/1253563209462448241",
        "images": "https://cdn.elizaos.news/elizaos-media/embed-thumbnail-1536621378000654397_e4308d42.jpg",
        "videos": "https://cdn.elizaos.news/elizaos-media/4g6klntgkxw_55f3736a.mp4"
      },
      {
        "text": "Community members expressed significant skepticism and frustration. Several users labeled the project a scam, with some accusing insiders of continuing to sell tokens at depressed prices. Concerns were raised about Bybit threatening to delist the ElizaOS token, with the exchange already applying an ST tag indicating low liquidity. One user noted that ElizaOS is increasingly resembling a memecoin but lacks the strong community typically associated with one. Another user suggested Shaw may be pivoting toward MetaDAO, which some interpreted as signaling the end of the ElizaOS token era, with Eliza continuing only as an open framework rather than a token-backed project.",
        "sources": "https://discord.com/channels/1253563208833433701/1253563209462448241",
        "images": "https://cdn.elizaos.news/elizaos-media/embed-thumbnail-1536724619351031928_84be656d.jpg"
      },
      {
        "text": "A separate video was shared showing Shaw Walters participating in a panel titled AI In Open Systems: Native Participation, Verifiability, and the Future of Autonomous Finance at the Future of Digital Currency Initiative Stanford Summit 2026, alongside Christian Catalini from the MIT Cryptoeconomics Lab and Professor Ruizhe Jia from Stanford University. Some community members noted they were still following Shaw's work and activities despite the token controversy. A small number of members expressed continued confidence that development work was ongoing.",
        "sources": "https://discord.com/channels/1253563208833433701/1253563209462448241",
        "images": "https://cdn.elizaos.news/elizaos-media/embed-thumbnail-1536848793834889217_a8445101.jpg",
        "videos": "https://cdn.elizaos.news/elizaos-media/f_rsiwldrx0_74e800f6.mp4"
      },
      {
        "text": "The coders channel had no activity recorded for this date.",
        "sources": "https://discord.com/channels/1253563208833433701/1300025221834739744",
        "posters": "https://cdn.elizaos.news/posters/1786496201705-643ojh.jpg"
      }
    ],
